  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nUpper level ridging will be in place across eastern New York \nand western New England through Thursday. High pressure slides \noff to the east today allowing for a south to southwesterly \nreturn flow to return to the region. This will allow for a \ngradual increase in temperatures as well as low level moisture. \nNear normal high temperatures in the 50s to lower 60s today \nwill trend above normal to the 60s to near 70 by Thursday. A \nshortwave tracking up and over the ridge will bring some mid \nand high clouds today into tonight but no precipitation is \nexpected. The only chance for any light showers will occur \nThursday night as a cold front crosses the region. Moisture will\nbe limited with the best forcing remaining well to the north \nacross Canada. Still, some moisture getting picked up from the \nGreat Lakes should allow for some light rain showers, mainly for\nareas north and west of Albany. There is the chance a few rain \nshowers may extend eastward into the Capital District and some \npoints south and east, but not enough confidence to include any \nPoPs at this time. Any rainfall amounts will be light and under \n0.10 inches. Drier weather returns regionwide Friday into next \nweekend as another high pressure system builds overhead with \nupper level flow becoming more zonal. Friday looks to be breezy \nbehind the cold front with some gusts 25 to 30 mph, especially \nfrom the Mohawk Valley into the Capital District and Berkshires.\nModel spread increases significantly for early next week \nleading to low confidence on when our next best chance for more \nwidespread rainfall occurs.",
